CAT = "cehrsCompliance"; SEG = "REG"
Q = [
("Which of these, kept with a patient's visit record, counts as protected health information?",
 "Her next of kin's name", ["The clinic's address", "The provider's NPI", "The EHR vendor's name"],
 "Identifiers such as a next of kin's name, phone number or email address become PHI when held with health information about the patient."),
("Under HIPAA's safe harbor method, what may be kept from a patient's date of birth in a de-identified data set?",
 'The year only', ['The month and year', 'The full date', 'Nothing at all'],
 'Safe harbor removes all elements of dates except the year for dates tied to a person.'),
('Under safe harbor, how must the age of a 93-year-old be shown in a de-identified data set?',
 'As 90 or older', ['As 93 exactly', 'As over 65', 'As the birth year'],
 'Ages over 89 must be removed, though they may be grouped into a single category of 90 or older.'),
('Under safe harbor de-identification, when may the first three digits of a ZIP code stay?',
 'If that area has over 20,000 people', ['When the patient agrees in writing', 'When the ZIP is in the same state as the clinic', 'Never; ZIP codes are always removed'],
 'The first three digits may stay only if the combined area for those digits has more than 20,000 people; otherwise they become 000.'),
("Besides safe harbor, what is HIPAA's other method of de-identifying data?",
 'Expert determination', ['Patient consent', 'Data encryption', 'Password protection rules'],
 'A qualified expert can apply statistical methods and document that the risk of re-identification is very small.'),
('A data set keeps town, state and ZIP code but removes names and record numbers. It may be shared for research only with which agreement?',
 'A data use agreement', ['A business associate agreement', 'A notice of privacy practices', 'A non-compete agreement'],
 'This is a limited data set, which may be used for research, public health or operations under a data use agreement.'),
('A coworker asks to borrow your EHR login for a minute. What is the right response?',
 'Decline politely', ['Agree but stay beside her', 'Agree if her account is locked', 'Log in for her yourself'],
 'Sharing credentials breaks accountability: every entry made under your login is recorded as yours. Each user works under a unique ID.'),
('Which behavior is a threat to the security of electronic information?',
 'Leaving a workstation logged in', ['Locking the screen when stepping away', 'Using a privacy screen', 'Changing a password when prompted'],
 "An unattended, logged-in workstation lets anyone view or change ePHI under that user's name. The others are safeguards."),
('Why do front desk monitors often have privacy screens?',
 'So nearby people cannot read them', ['So the screen locks sooner', 'So users can skip logging off at lunch', 'So the monitor meets fire code'],
 'Privacy filters narrow the viewing angle, so PHI on screen is not visible to people nearby.'),
('Which password practice best follows common security policy?',
 'A long passphrase not used elsewhere', ['A short password that is changed daily', 'Your birth date plus a symbol', 'A password reused from email'],
 'Long, unique passwords kept private resist guessing; reusing or writing them down defeats them.'),
('An EHR automatically logs a user out after 10 minutes without activity. Under the Security Rule, this is which kind of specification?',
 'Addressable', ['Required', 'Optional', 'Prohibited by law'],
 'Automatic logoff is an addressable implementation specification under 45 CFR 164.312: the entity must assess it and adopt it or document a reasonable alternative.'),
("Under the Security Rule's technical safeguards, which access control specification is required?",
 'Unique user identification', ['Automatic logoff', 'Encryption and decryption', 'Fingerprint readers'],
 'Unique user identification and emergency access procedures are required. Automatic logoff and encryption are addressable.'),
('Which Security Rule standard records and examines activity in systems that hold ePHI?',
 'Audit controls', ['Integrity controls', 'Facility access', 'Workforce training'],
 'Audit controls (45 CFR 164.312(b)) are the hardware, software or procedures that record and examine activity in information systems.'),
("A front desk clerk's EHR account can view schedules and demographics but not psychiatry notes. What principle is this?",
 'Role-based access', ['Open access', 'Single sign-on', 'Shared team access'],
 "Role-based privileges give each user only the access their job needs, which also supports HIPAA's minimum necessary standard."),
('An employee moves from billing to scheduling. What should happen to her EHR access?',
 'Change it to match the new role', ['Add scheduling to her billing access', 'Keep billing until her review', 'Suspend all access for 30 days'],
 'Access is updated when a role changes so she keeps only what the new job needs.'),
('A compliance check finds an account still active for someone who left two months ago. What is the right action?',
 'Disable it and report the gap', ['Reset its password', 'Rename it for a new hire', 'Leave it active until HR confirms'],
 'Accounts of departed staff are removed promptly; an active one is a security gap to close and report.'),
('A breach of unsecured PHI affects 650 patients. When must HHS be told?',
 'With the notice to the patients', ['Within 60 days after the calendar year ends', 'Only if the media reports it', 'Within one year'],
 'For 500 or more people, HHS is notified at the same time as the individuals, per 45 CFR 164.408.'),
('A breach affects 12 patients. How is it reported to HHS?',
 'In a yearly log after year-end', ['Immediately by phone', 'Only to the state, not HHS', 'Not reported to HHS at all'],
 'Breaches of fewer than 500 people are logged and reported to HHS no later than 60 days after the end of the calendar year in which they were discovered.'),
('A breach notice to patients must include which element?',
 'Steps patients can take', ['The name of the employee at fault', "Each patient's full record", "The clinic's cyber insurer"],
 'Under 45 CFR 164.404 the notice describes what happened, what PHI was involved, steps individuals should take and what the entity is doing, with contact information.'),
('A patient asks for her records to be sent directly to her new doctor. What must her request be?',
 'In writing and signed, naming who', ["Made by her new doctor's office", 'Made in person with a photo ID shown', 'Notarized and signed'],
 'Under the right of access, a direction to send a copy to another person must be in writing, signed and clearly name the recipient and where to send it.'),
('A patient requests her records in electronic form. What must the practice provide if it is readily producible?',
 'The electronic form she asked for', ['A paper copy of the record', 'A summary written by the staff instead', 'Access to the staff EHR'],
 'If the requested electronic form and format can readily be produced, it must be provided; otherwise an agreed readable electronic format.'),
('A practice cannot finish a records access request within 30 days. How long may one extension last?',
 'Up to 30 more days', ['Up to 10 more days', 'Up to 60 more days', 'As long as needed'],
 'One extension of up to 30 days is allowed if the patient is told the reason and the new date in writing before the first deadline.'),
("A practice denies a patient's request to amend her chart because it is accurate. What may she then file?",
 'A statement of disagreement', ['A new medical record number', 'A business associate agreement', 'A prior authorization'],
 'She may file a statement of disagreement, which is attached to the record and included with later disclosures.'),
('How far back can an accounting of disclosures cover?',
 'Six years before the request', ['One year before the request', 'Ten years before the request', "Since the patient's birth"],
 'An individual may request an accounting of disclosures covering up to six years before the request.'),
('Which disclosure does not have to appear in an accounting of disclosures?',
 'One made for treatment', ['One made to a court under order', 'One made to a researcher with a waiver', 'One made to report abuse'],
 'Disclosures for treatment, payment and health care operations, to the patient, or under her authorization are excluded from the accounting.'),
("Under HIPAA, what may a practice charge for an individual's first accounting of disclosures in a 12-month period?",
 'Nothing', ['A cost-based fee', 'A per-page copy fee', 'A flat records fee'],
 'The first accounting in any 12-month period is free; a reasonable cost-based fee may apply to later requests if the patient is told in advance.'),
("Which act's rules stop practices that interfere with access to, exchange or use of electronic health information?",
 'The 21st Century Cures Act', ['The Affordable Care Act', 'The Privacy Act of 1974', 'The Clinical Laboratory Improvement Act'],
 "The Cures Act's information blocking provisions, in 45 CFR part 171, apply to providers, certified health IT developers and health information networks or exchanges."),
('Which is one of the information blocking exceptions?',
 'Preventing harm', ['Saving staff time', 'Protecting market share', 'Avoiding patient questions'],
 'The exceptions include preventing harm, privacy, security, infeasibility, health IT performance, protecting care access, manner, fees, licensing and TEFCA manner.'),
("A provider types an abbreviation that is not on the organization's approved list. What should the note show instead?",
 'The term written out in full', ['The abbreviation in capitals', 'The abbreviation plus a footnote', 'An approved symbol instead'],
 'Organizations keep an approved abbreviation list and a do-not-use list; anything not approved is spelled out so no reader misinterprets it.'),
('Before a planned EHR outage, what should staff have ready?',
 'Downtime forms and recent reports', ['A list of vendor contacts', 'Extra printer toner', 'New passwords ready for all staff members'],
 'Downtime procedures include paper forms and recent reports, such as schedules and medication lists, so care continues; data is entered once the system returns.'),
('Which part of a HIPAA contingency plan restores lost EHR data?',
 'The disaster recovery plan', ['The notice of privacy practices', 'The sanctions policy', 'The minimum necessary policy'],
 "The Security Rule's contingency plan includes a data backup plan, a disaster recovery plan and an emergency mode operation plan."),
("Under CMS's Promoting Interoperability measures, which activity must clinicians attest they conducted or reviewed?",
 'A security risk analysis', ['A patient satisfaction survey', 'A clinical quality audit', 'A disaster drill'],
 'The Promoting Interoperability measures require conducting or reviewing a security risk analysis under the HIPAA Security Rule; without it the category earns no score.'),
]
